dialect grammar
grammar stdio.md.snapshot
extension md
start document
comment none

# P2-FG1: real (honest-subset) CommonMark grammar of `stdio.md`'s wire text -- replaces the
# pre-Phase-2 ABNF placeholder that was unparseable by the repo's own `parse_grammar` (wrong header
# dialect entirely: `dialect grammar stdio.md.snapshot` on one line, `;` comments, `/` alternation,
# `%x`/`0*3SP` char-class quantifiers -- none of them in this dialect's lexer alphabet). `comment
# none` is MANDATORY here: an ATX heading starts its line with `#`, which this dialect's shared
# lexer treats as a LINE-COMMENT marker by default (would silently swallow the whole heading line as
# trivia) -- the same fix csv's/txt's own real grammars needed for their own meaningful leading
# characters (recipe §1.1). With `comment none`, `#` instead lexes as an ordinary one-char `Error`
# token (`🔍️lexer/🦀️.rs`'s own `comment_line_marker_is_configurable_and_disableable` test),
# matchable by an ordinary `Symbol::Literal("#")` (`Literal` compares TOKEN TEXT only, never token
# KIND -- `📖️grammar/🦀️.rs`'s `match_symbol_tracked`).
#
# ⚠️ MECHANISM GAP (P2-W0 §1a, EXPLICITLY EXCLUDED -- not fixed here, not worked around cleverly):
# CommonMark's LEADING-WHITESPACE-COUNT block nesting -- list-item dedent (`list_item_marker`'s own
# `indent+marker_len` continuation-line width, `⚙️engine/🦀️.rs:87-104,283-350`) -- is
# architecturally impossible for this shared TOKEN-based, whitespace-as-TRIVIA lexer: whitespace
# never becomes a token at all, so no grammar production can ever count it. `MdBlock::List` is
# therefore NOT modeled below (no `list`/`list-item` production exists anywhere in this file). This
# does NOT weaken round-trip correctness: `render_markdown_blocks` (the real `print_dsl`/
# `encode_pack` encoder) ALWAYS re-emits code blocks FENCED, never 4-space-indented
# (`⚙️engine/🦀️.rs`'s own documented normal form) -- 4-space indented code literally never
# appears in real rendered output either, so that half of the excluded gap costs nothing here.
# `MdBlock::HtmlBlock` is ALSO left unmodeled: its real end condition ("next blank line") is
# LINE-COUNT-bounded the same fragile way `paragraph` below is (no `NEWLINE` terminal exists to
# delimit it -- `csv-newline-trivia` gap, restated), and it is not one of the 5 kinds this wave's own
# recipe brief names as in-scope (ATX headings, fenced code blocks, thematic breaks, block quotes,
# paragraph text) -- documented scope cut, not silently dropped.
#
# `FENCE` is this dialect's own BUILT-IN raw-multi-line-span token
# (`🔍️lexer/🦀️.rs`'s `Shape::Embed`-only special case, ```lang\ncontent\n``` scanned as ONE
# token) -- it matches `render_block`'s `MdBlock::CodeBlock` output (always exactly 3 backticks,
# never indented, never tilde-fenced -- `⚙️engine`'s documented normal form) almost exactly; the
# "mismatched from CommonMark's own fence rule" caveat P2-W0 §1a records only bites content that
# ITSELF needs a bare `` ``` `` closing line inside a block quote's `"> "`-prefixed rendering, which
# this grammar's demo fixture (below) deliberately avoids.
#
# `LINE`/`REST` (P2-M1 item 2) capture `Heading`/`Paragraph`'s prose content verbatim past their
# marker, honestly NOT recursively re-parsing `MdInline` structure at this layer -- the recipe's own
# explicit guidance for this wave. The real `MdInline`/`MdBlock` recursion IS exercised genuinely,
# with a typed `Ref` self-recursion matching json's own `value` precedent, by the sibling
# `../../🧬️mutations/📝️text/📖️.grammar.semio` /
# `../../🔺️diff/📝️text/📖️.grammar.semio` wire grammars (both walk the identical
# `md-block`/`md-inline` shape `enc_block`/`enc_inline` in
# `../../🔺️diff/🦀️.rs` actually emit).
#
# `paragraph` is modeled as EXACTLY ONE physical `LINE`, never `LINE+`: `Symbol::Plus`/`Symbol::Star`
# never backtrack (§3 pitfall #2), and this dialect has no `NEWLINE` terminal to bound how many
# consecutive lines belong to one paragraph vs. the next block -- a `LINE+` paragraph would greedily
# swallow the ENTIRE REST of the document as "more paragraph" (`LINE` always succeeds while any
# token remains), never stopping at a following block's own marker. A genuine multi-line paragraph
# (produced by an embedded `SoftBreak`/`HardBreak` inline) is consequently NOT grammar-recognizable
# here -- `⚙️engine/🦀️.rs`'s own `demo_md_snapshot()` fixture (used by this wave's
# `grammar_conformance_law`/`fixture_honesty_law`) deliberately avoids one, same documented-scope-cut
# category as `List`/`HtmlBlock` above.
#
# `block-quote` IS genuinely recursive (`Ref(block)`) -- CommonMark's `>` marker is a real per-line
# KEYWORD/promoted-token check (M1's promoted `GT` terminal), not an indentation COUNT, so it does
# NOT hit the excluded gap above. Each `GT block` iteration recognizes one already-rendered
# `"> "`-prefixed physical line as one recursive `block` (matches `render_block`'s own per-inner-line
# `"> "`-prefixing of its flattened `render_blocks` output, `⚙️engine/🦀️.rs:657-669`) --
# proven end-to-end against a genuinely 2-level-nested `BlockQuote{BlockQuote{Paragraph}}` fixture by
# this wave's own `grammar_conformance_law`. A block quote wrapping MULTIPLE sibling blocks (which
# renders internal blank `"> "` lines between them) is NOT recognizable this way -- an empty
# continuation of `"> "` with nothing left on that physical line has no next same-line token for
# `LINE` to bound itself against, so it would incorrectly swallow the FOLLOWING quoted line instead
# -- `demo_md_snapshot()` therefore always nests exactly one block per block-quote level.
#
# 🐛 A SECOND, independent gotcha (a genuine shared-LEXER BUG, not this grammar's own design, filed
# as `mechanism_gaps` id `md-fence-byte-offset-corruption`, non-blocking, worked around locally):
# `🔍️lexer/🦀️.rs`'s `FENCE`-token scanning loop increments its running `byte_offset` for
# every consumed char EXCEPT `'\n'` -- so every token positioned AFTER a fenced block whose content
# spans N lines gets its OWN `byte_range` under-reported by N bytes, which can desync
# `match_raw_span`'s `text.get(start_byte..).find('\n')` lookup enough to make a `LINE`/`REST` match
# immediately following the fence silently zero-width (confirmed by direct reproduction: a `FENCE`
# then `block-quote`'s own nested `paragraph = LINE` consistently breaks this way; `atx-heading`/
# `paragraph`/`thematic-break` placed directly after a fence were empirically NOT observed to trip
# it for the specific layouts tested, but the underlying corruption is real and general, not
# layout-specific luck worth depending on). `⚙️engine/🦀️.rs`'s `demo_md_snapshot()`
# deliberately places `CodeBlock` LAST, followed only by `ThematicBreak` (a pure-literal-token
# production that never calls `LINE`/`REST`, so it's structurally immune) -- not a fix to the shared
# lexer (out of this wave's ownership boundary), just an honest layout choice that avoids the trap.
artifact-mark = "stdio.md"
document = artifact-mark block*

block = thematic-break | fenced-code-block | block-quote | atx-heading | paragraph

# `render_block`'s `MdBlock::ThematicBreak` always emits the fixed literal `"---\n\n"` -- `"-"` alone
# lexes as `Minus`, but two consecutive `-` characters fuse into one `DashArrow` token (text `"--"`,
# `🔍️lexer/🦀️.rs`'s own dash-fusion rule) BEFORE the third `-` is reached, so `"---"`
# tokenizes as `DashArrow("--")` then `Minus("-")` -- two literal tokens, not three.
thematic-break = "--" "-"

fenced-code-block = FENCE

block-quote = {GT block}+

# `"#"` (0-6 of them, `render_block`'s own `.clamp(1,6)`) then the heading's rendered inline text,
# verbatim, to end of line.
atx-heading = "#"+ LINE

paragraph = LINE
